Fix read.dcf() re-encoding a whole field value for every continuation
line once it contains a 4-byte sequence above U+10FFFF, which made the
dcf target time out.

mkCharUTF8sub() in src/main/dcf.c gates on utf8Valid() and repairs via
iconv() with "<xx>" substitution.  glibc and libiconv both pass
well-formed 4-byte sequences for code points above U+10FFFF (lead bytes
F4 90.. to F7) through unchanged, while utf8Valid() rejects them
(RFC 3629).  Such a value never satisfies the gate, so each further
continuation line of that field re-encodes the whole accumulated value
again.  A 40 KB record with one such line followed by ~6000
continuation lines took 4.5 s on a release build and past the 25 s
libFuzzer limit on the fuzzing bot:

    ==252== ERROR: libFuzzer: timeout after 35 seconds
       #19 R_FreeStringBuffer      src/main/memory.c:4988
       #20 Rf_reEnc4               src/main/sysutils.c:2407
       #21 Rf_reEnc3               src/main/sysutils.c:2394
       #22 mkCharUTF8sub           src/main/dcf.c:51
       #23 do_readDCF              src/main/dcf.c:261
       ...
       #42 LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput  harnesses/dcf.c:56

Escape invalid bytes with utf8Valid()'s own rules instead (a small
utf8ValidClen() helper next to utf8Valid() in src/main/util.c), so the
result is valid by construction and later lines take the fast path.
The "<xx>" escapes are the same ones iconv(sub = "byte") produces for
everything it does reject.  With the change the input takes 0.28 s.

Found by fuzzing R with libFuzzer + AddressSanitizer (OSS-Fuzz dcf
target, testcase 4718311766818816).  The slow path is in R-devel only
(r90200 and later); R 4.6.1 is not affected.

Upstream status: r-devel/r-svn PR (to be opened); no R Bugzilla report
yet.  The upstream patch also adds a regression test to
tests/reg-tests-1e.R, left out here so this carry patch does not go
stale with that file.  The dcf.c hunk is made against a tree with
patches 0015 and 0021 applied, which also change that file.  Delete
this patch once the fix lands in R trunk (ossfuzz.sh will report it as
already-applied in the meantime, which is harmless).

